SFS legacy seek regression
==========================

Build sfs_seek through test-dos-common or with the target AROS C compiler.
Mount a disposable SFS volume as SFST: with at least 2200000000 free bytes.
Run sfs_seek with stdout redirected to a log on another volume. It refuses
an existing SFST:audit-seek-regression.tmp and deletes its own test file.
Do not run two copies at once. An interrupted test may leave its file behind.
This test does not format a disk or install a handler.

The test writes and reopens a 16-byte file, then a 2049 MiB file. Each seek
case opens a fresh handle, so zero is the expected old position on success.
It checks absolute, current and end-relative positions, reads after seeking,
and rejects negative or out-of-range destinations. The unmodified handler
rejects five valid destinations in the large file with ERROR_SEEK_ERROR.
A handler with only the unsigned size comparison still fails the wraparound
cases: end/current-relative addition must not wrap past 4 GiB. The test checks
resulting positions after ordinary seeks and unchanged position after failures.
For a write handle already above LONG_MAX, an EOF read checks that a rejected
current-relative seek leaves it at EOF.
The corrected handler must finish with failures=0 and exit code 0.
Exit 10 means a failed assertion; exit 20 means setup, I/O or cleanup failed.

This tests destinations representable as nonnegative LONG values only.
It does not establish 64-bit seek support or change the handling of offsets
above LONG_MAX. It requires about 2 GiB of physical writes per execution.
